{"assets":["/editorial-assets/aa329784ad00c6d045a4041a294b816573983483bf976a4feefd418d3510b00c.png","/editorial-assets/6ed0e25d3df67939a3bd4705f61d7fde04c56d37aad2ed8661d8b0d88e26ef4c.png","/editorial-assets/6d48860413f5b5265356126ed57a1dad8aceae24b393aedd207048fc9ac92cdc.png","/editorial-assets/ff7a0baa9f5a65d69c16562b1f261f811e8615aaa01fbc506074914d976208e0.png","/editorial-assets/9db6e4e979aca8d272b76f6a59742d89e7104dddb7e46ef90f5b9c6928537ffc.png","/editorial-assets/23c3e4f1a5fef4575e70ff8af00a7603fa964e026d304ffa0a0891afbb7eac10.png","/editorial-assets/2588a5d6139607d01329f835cd4cfa56f22cc73c5883a42979b52fe4048b879b.png","/editorial-assets/a815b3a9421ee905e985637263e0b7acbee6e89ac42cb5ae34569704edcf38f7.png","/editorial-assets/1266602b7544ab7716fbd89dd490a1a36e8bb0eff1c74dffe99683103b694513.png","/editorial-assets/c7704a1140cb450ba5f555e396ed6aa600d3760bc2343d91383f750fdd53be81.jpg","/editorial-assets/b432c23d334e09047956d9416408ed84e7b9667078e9ccc415a7466093cfb2a5.png","/editorial-assets/05551a042268291669571dbe35101a8bdc5dcce0412f9045d56ed53d1094a765.png","/editorial-assets/52d6b7a1a0df2f16bcf71323e03831447d13998bb7a9832365b189fe811ef6b6.png","/editorial-assets/7e80b11713198ef3295e59c065250333609636f80ab09a5283e830d413632873.png","/editorial-assets/be3573643e1aa89c30b87c5a5671be9064805cd7c4a048bce5e481c3f5231725.png","/editorial-assets/a9e4f1e7e5fb4065bb120b035c9f34f7bafb47f6c055aa381b759d26c3fda985.png","/editorial-assets/c3c33d60b2899d7e701290dab626735fa3e9fbe25b5d2b3cc5b5a344ee671cf0.png","/editorial-assets/2bbb6b6e37fa971cf804520948ea8e5d3b11c786e2c3bbd7bbad1f23420d92d1.png"],"contestId":"194","diagnostics":[{"code":"editorial-asset-unsupported","message":"Replaced unsupported image source","path":"document/35/0","severity":"warning"}],"document":{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Please, report me about every grammar mistake, you see. Thank you."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem <>"}],"kind":"strong"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's notice that 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ 5"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < 3"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" author has to get 2 on some exams. There are 3"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" such exams and that's the answer). If 3"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" author will pass all exams (answer is 0)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem <>"}],"kind":"strong"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let the pencil move by the line and put crosses through every ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1) point. Lets associate every point on the line with point on square perimeter. Namely, point "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" on the line will be associated with point on square perimeter that we will reach if we move pencil around square to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" clockwise. Then lower left corner will be associated with all points 4"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"np"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" for every non-negative integer "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Crosses will be associated with points "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1) for some non-negative "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Nearest point of overlap of two families of points will be in point LCM("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1, 4"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). Then we will put "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/aa329784ad00c6d045a4041a294b816573983483bf976a4feefd418d3510b00c.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" crosses."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem \"Cutting Figure\""}],"kind":"strong"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Translation by "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/fdoer"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"fdoer"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Main idea: using the fact that the answer cannot be greater than 2, check answer 1."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's proof that the answer is not greater than 2. Let area of the figure be greater than 3. Let's examine the leftmost of all topmost squares. There is no neighbors left or up to it. So, number of its neighbors is not more than 2. Thus, if we delete its neighbors, we shall disconnect the figure. If the area is equal to 3, we always can disconnect the figure by deletion of one square. It can be proofed by considering all two primary cases. If the area is not greater than 2, there is no way to disconnect the figure."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The algorithm: Check answer 1. We can simply brute-force the square to delete, and for each instance start dfs from any existing square. If during the dfs we visited not all of the remaining squares, we have found the square to delete. The answer is 1 if our bruteforce succeeded, and 2 otherwise."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"That was necessary to consider the case when there is no answer."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The complexity of the described algorithm is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"4"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"). That was possible to search for articulation points and solve the problem in complexity "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"), but in my opinion that required more time and effort."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem <>"}],"kind":"strong"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Translation by "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/fdoer"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"fdoer"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Main idea: bruteforce in complexity "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"F"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"F"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" if fibonacci number at position "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"This problem had complex statements. We have an array "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", and we can transform it in two ways. The goal was to maximize the sum of all its elements with given multipliers after exactly "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" operations. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A simple bruteforce of all combinations of operations with subsequent modeling leads to complexity "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nu"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"), what is not fast enough. That was possible to optimize it by modeling parallel to recursive bruteforce. Now we have complexity "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). Actually, the correct solution is not too far from this algorithm."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"There is just one conjecture: every two successive xor operations change nothing, and we can move them to any place of the combination. Thus, it will be enough to bruteforce only combinations in which every pair of successive xor operations is at the end."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"It could be done using recoursive bruteforce. We must change in previous solution two things. First, we must n't put xor after xor. Besides that, we should update answer if number "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is even, where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is current level of recoursion (all remaining operations in the end separates to pairs of xors)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's calculate complexity of this algo. There are "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"F"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" sequences of length "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" without two consecutive xors. It's easy to proof, you can calculate some dp to see it. That's why, complexity of our algo is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"F"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem \"Hamming distance\""}],"kind":"strong"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Main idea: reduction to system of linear equations and solving it using Gauss algorithm."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's notice that order of columns in answer doesn't matter. That's why there is only one important thing — quantity of every type of column. There is only 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"4"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 16 different columns. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's represent Hamming distance between every pair of strings as sum of quantities of types of columns. It's possible because every column adds to every distance between pairs 0 or 1. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now we have system of 6 linear equations with 16 variables. It's not good, let's decrease number of variables. First, some columns adds same values to every Hamming distance. For example strings \"abbb\" and \"baaa\". For every column "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"q"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" we can replace all letters \"a\" by letters \"b\" and all letters \"b\" by letters \"a\" and reach column that adds same values to every distance. We reduced number of variables to 8. We also can notice that columns \"aaaa\" and \"bbbb\" is useless and reduce number of variables to 7."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"This system can be solved using Gauss algorithm. One variable steel be free. Let's fix it. It's value can't be more than maximum of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"h"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") because column adds positive value to one or more Hamming distance. For every fixed value we should check if all variables take non-negative integer value and choose the best answer."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We can solve system of equations in integers because coefficients of equation is little."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Complexity of this solution if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"max"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"h"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"))). If we solve it in rational numbers complexity will change to "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/6ed0e25d3df67939a3bd4705f61d7fde04c56d37aad2ed8661d8b0d88e26ef4c.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem \"Two segments\""}],"kind":"strong"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Main idea: inverse the permutation and solve simplified problem (see below), consider function \"quantity of segments of permutation that form the given segment of natural series\"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In order to solve this problem, we suggest solve another: <<we have a permutation "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", we have to calculate the count of segments such that their elements form one or two segments of natural series>>."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If we solve the inverse problem for some permutation "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"q"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" such that "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/6d48860413f5b5265356126ed57a1dad8aceae24b393aedd207048fc9ac92cdc.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", we shall get the answer for the initial problem and initial permutation "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Straight-forward algo: let's bruteforce the segment of permutation and mark its elements in a boolean array. Check that in that array there is not more than two marked segments. This algo has complexity "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's notice that during the changeover from ["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] to ["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1] the quantity of segments changes in some predictable way. Let "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]) be quantity of segments that form segment ["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] of permutation. There are three cases (see picture below):"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":false},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If the new element "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" is between two marked elements (that is, both elements with values "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1 and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1 belong to segment ["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]), then "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1]) = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]) - 1. The new element will <> the segments near it."}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If the new element "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" has only one neighbor with value belonging to ["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"], then "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text"